def add(n,item,scans,lines,his,statement,status,cls,fate,refs,confidence='high',comp=None,date=NOW,present=None):
    file='K29-02_transcription.tex' if max(scans)<=202 else 'K29-03_transcription.tex'
    C.append(dict(id=f'CL-SA12-{n}',items=[f'SA12-{x}' for x in ([item] if isinstance(item,int) else item)],cote=29,scans=scans,lines=file+':'+lines,his_statement=his,statement=statement,status_on_pages=status,computation=comp or 'No numerical calculation underlies this claim. Hypotheses and proof scope were read; any independent proof is separately recorded in CHECK_EVENTS.tsv and proofs.md.',**{'class':cls},fate=fate,fate_date=date,present_status='As of November2026: '+(present or fate),refs=refs,confidence=confidence))
add(801,801,[188],'13–41','φ_n is the displayed symmetric polynomial; P d′′f=f−U_1⋯U_nf.','With e_k elementary symmetric and φ_n=(1/n)Σ_{k=0}^{n−1}e_k/binom(n−1,k), Σ_j(1−x_j)φ_n(x with x_j=0)=1−∏x_j. Combined with Cauchy–Green, this gives the displayed function identity on a smooth product domain, conditionally identifying ψ_n with φ_n.','stated without proof; entire block cancelled','F','Cauchy–Green is classical and is matched to Demailly I(3.2), but this exact symmetric coefficient formula was not identified in the searched sources. Independently proved by a beta-integral telescoping identity in proofs.md §1. Cancellation has no documented explanation.',['SA12_Demailly2012','SA12_SerreCartan1954'],comp='Exact rational polynomial checks for n=1,…,8 in programs/exact_checks.py; outputs/exact_checks.txt. The integral argument in proofs.md proves every n.',present='The algebraic identity is correct under the stated polynomial interpretation. The analytical domain and ψ/φ identification are reconstructed; no transcription is changed.')
add(802,801,[188],'28–41','L’opérateur d’homotopie … Pω=φ_n(U;J)Σ(−1)^{ν−1}T_{jν}a dbar z_{J\setminus jν}.','For smooth (0,q)-forms on a neighbourhood of a closed polydisc, the reconstructed P satisfies d′′P+Pd′′=1 for q>0; qφ_J+Σ_{k∉J}(1−x_k)φ_{J∪{k}}=1 controls its diagonal terms.','proof absent; cancelled working definition','E','The local d′′-Poincaré lemma is already documented by Serre’s1953–1954 Cartan exposé18 §§2–3, citing Dolbeault’s1953 note. Demailly I§3.E(3.29) gives the modern lemma. The manuscript’s particular symmetrization was not historically identified; its full homotopy is our check.',['SA12_Demailly2012','SA12_SerreCartan1954'],comp='Exact positive-degree coefficient identities for every1≤q≤n≤8 in outputs/exact_checks.txt; general integral and operator proof in proofs.md §1.',date='Dolbeault1953 note cited in the opened contemporary1953–1954 exposé; Demailly manuscript revision21June2012. No date is assigned to188.')
add(803,803,[191],'48–78','r commute aux sommes finies et au passage au quotient … exact à droite … mais … pas exact à gauche.','A realization of a ramification datum preserves finite coproducts and the stated admissible equivalence-relation quotients. This is a right-exactness requirement, not preservation of arbitrary finite limits. R2–R4 separately control clopens, effective-descent covers and rank strata.','axiomatic definition with forward reference','C','Finite-group quotients and Galois categories are developed in SGA1 V1,4–6. The exact stack-plus-realization axiom package R1–R4 was not located by title/phrase searches or backwards references from Borne and Grothendieck–Murre; no claim of nondevelopment is made.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Borne2009','SA12_GrothendieckMurre1971'],date=SGA)
add(804,803,[192],'83–97','mettre en lemme: r commute aux produits fibrés X_i×_X X′.','A finite-coproduct-preserving realization between an extensive finite-cover category and schemes preserves pullbacks along complemented monomorphisms.','proof sketched; lemma requested','A','This is the elementary summand calculus underlying SGA1 V finite-cover formalism; independently re-proved from universal complementary decompositions in proofs.md §2. It does not license an arbitrary inverse-image-site theorem for r.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Mathew2016'],date=SGA)
add(805,[803,804,805],[191,192],'75–103','ce sont les familles couvrantes d’une topologie … moins fine que la canonique.','Under R1–R4, real-surjective families form a pullback-stable coverage after reducing to images and summands; R3 gives subcanonicity, R4 ensures the needed rank-piece covers, and base changes on the étale base are continuous.','proof sketched','C','The coverage argument was checked using CL804 and quotient-surjectivity. Mathew’s Definition5.15 and local-triviality calculus support the finite-cover mechanism, but neither it nor SGA1 supplies this exact non-left-exact realization-topology statement verbatim. No exact SGA4 reference requested on192 was identified.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Mathew2016'],confidence='medium',present='The stated coverage construction has a coherent proof under the prescribed admissible quotient and effective-descent hypotheses. Its attribution to one exact published SGA4 result remains unidentified.')
add(806,804,[192],'99–103','les topos associés … un champ en topos; R_{S′} … objets loc. constants finis.','The associated subcanonical site should recover the original category as its finite locally constant objects, compatibly with descent over Et(S), so as to form a stack of topoi.','stated without proof','C','Local finite-cover reconstruction is standard categorical Galois/descent theory; Mathew §§5.2–5.4 gives a precise bounded-rank analogue, and Stacks0BLZ verifies the finite-étale-cover stack. The full arbitrary multigaloisian topos-stack assertion and its coherence are not proved on192 or identified with a single opened theorem.',['SA12_Mathew2016','SA12_Stacks0BLZ','SA12_SGA1'],confidence='medium',date=MAT,present='Partly matched: finite-cover descent and local reconstruction are established, but the exact stack-of-topoi formulation requires a stated 2-categorical descent framework.')
add(807,806,[193],'104–108','X=∅ ⇔ r(X)=∅; X connexe ⇔ r(X) connexe.','R1 and R2 force detection of the initial object and of nonempty connected objects by the bijection between complementary decompositions and clopens.','stated without proof','A','An elementary consequence of the finite-cover/summand formalism of SGA1 V; the proof is independently supplied in proofs.md §2.',['SA12_SGA1'],date=SGA)
add(808,807,[193],'109–125','les objets subordonnés à P … stable … limites finies … sommandes.','Objects whose pullback to P is constant on each rank piece form a full subcategory closed under the indicated finite operations and summands; the étale-locally subordinate objects form a substack. For a covering G-torsor P, descent identifies them with piecewise constant objects over P with compatible G-action.','proof sketched; closure lemma requested','C','Galois trivializations and descent are matched in SGA1 V–VI and Mathew5.28,5.32. The printed definition permits P=0; the equivalence with data solely over P therefore needs the usual covering meaning of a Galois object. The separate literal final criterion fails in CL838.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Mathew2016'],confidence='medium',date=SGA)
add(809,809,[193,195],'123–125;132–138','le foncteur … est pl. fidèle … ess. surjectif … n’utilise pas l’hyp. galoisienne.','R1–R3 compare finite piecewise constant objects over P with piecewise constant schemes over r(P): R2 lifts clopen partitions and R3 descends the objects over those partitions.','proof sketched; lemma requested','C','The finite partition case follows directly by lifting clopens and finite coproducts. An unbounded rank partition uses the author’s effective-descent condition, not mere finite-colimit preservation. Classical descent is in SGA1 VI; Mathew’s version covers uniformly bounded rank.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Mathew2016'],confidence='medium')
add(810,809,[195],'139–145','Prop3 … équivalence … Th1 … localement subordonnés … condition automatique si Z fini sur S.','For a covering G-Galois P, X↦r(X×P) identifies subordinate objects with finite piecewise constant G-equivariant covers of Z=r(P); étale-local subordination corresponds to finite étale G-covers that become piecewise constant after étale base changes on S. If Z→S is finite, the latter local condition is automatic.','proof sketched via descent','A','SGA1 V–VI supplies Galois descent, and finite étale descent is stated in Stacks0BLZ. For the automatic condition, finite algebras over a strictly henselian local ring decompose into strictly henselian local factors, so finite étale covers split; Stacks04GK matches the henselian ingredient. Spread-out of finite-presentation data gives an étale neighbourhood.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BLZ','SA12_Stacks04GK'],confidence='high',date=SGA,present='Established under the finite-cover and covering-Galois-object interpretation. The text does not assert the condition for arbitrary infinite étale morphisms.')
add(811,810,[196],'146–155','Prop4 … quotient par G … font une donnée de ramification.','For a scheme Z/S with an admissible finite G-action, use G-equivariant finite étale covers locally piecewise constant after étale base change on S; realize them by their scheme quotients by G. Finite covers preserve the admissible affine-neighbourhood condition needed for those quotients.','proof sketched in abbreviated O.K. checks','C','Finite quotients and equivariant descent are classical (SGA1 V1 and VI); quotient-stack finite covers give the modern interpretation. This establishes the local quotient model under the source’s admissibility and finite-cover restrictions. It does not establish unrestricted scheme-valued gluing, refuted in CL817.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BLZ','SA12_Cadman2007'],confidence='medium')
add(812,810,[196,197],'156–169','rang … borné … limite inductive filtrante … Z_i≃Z_j/H_ij.','Under the stated quasi-compactness and bounded-rank/Galois-domination hypothesis, the category of global objects is a filtered union of Galois-generated subcategories. In the connected case, cofinal connected Galois objects yield surjective transition groups and quotient realizations.','proof sketched','A','SGA1 V4–5 gives cofinal Galois objects and the associated inverse systems; Mathew5.34–5.36 gives the filtered categorical version. This is a statement about the global category, not yet the entire stack.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Mathew2016'],date=SGA)
add(813,811,[197],'167–173','Prop5 …..; le champ … n’est pas nécessairement engendré par ses sections globales.','The global-object presentation need not generate a stack after all étale base changes; local models and gluing are necessary. The promised inverse-system converse Proposition5 is unwritten and has no recoverable full statement.','stated without proof; inverse-system converse left unwritten','F','SGA1 VI and the stack of finite covers support the local-versus-global distinction, but no exact theorem can be matched to an empty Proposition5. Searches of the title, R-axiom terminology and later Kummer references did not recover its missing quantifiers.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BLZ'],confidence='high',present='The limitation on global sections is valid; the unwritten Proposition5 is not a precisely identifiable claim.')
add(814,812,[198],'174–192','Prop6 … Hom … couples (P′,α).','A morphism from the datum modeled by (Z,G) is determined, up to the indicated natural isomorphisms, by a covering G-Galois object P′ in the target together with a G-equivariant identification r′(P′)≅Z.','proof sketched','C','Mathew Proposition5.32 gives the universal property of finite G-set categories in terms of G-torsors. Combining it with the opened finite-cover descent formalism explains the local model. The additional realization compatibility and the full relative stack version are specific to this draft and are not cited as a verbatim modern theorem.',['SA12_Mathew2016','SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BLZ'],confidence='medium',date=MAT)
add(815,812,[198],'190–194','φ est néc. pleinement fidèle.','An exact cartesian morphism of ramification data equipped with r′φ≅r induces bijections on subobjects by R2, hence is fully faithful by the graph/characteristic-map criterion.','proof sketched as corollary','A','SGA1 V6 supplies exact-functor criteria; this implication is independently re-proved using characteristic maps to1⊔1 and graph subobjects in proofs.md §8. It concerns φ, not the realization r.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BMQ'],date=SGA)
add(816,812,[198],'195–200','Hom … fibrés principaux P′ … P′/G′≃Z.','For two quotient models, morphisms correspond to G-torsors over Z′ with compatible G′-action, the local piecewise-constant condition on S, and an equivariant scheme isomorphism P′/G′≅Z.','stated without proof as corollary','C','The torsor universal property in Mathew5.32 and finite étale descent match the underlying mechanism. Quotient admissibility and the precise local-triviality restriction must remain in the statement; arbitrary equivariant torsors without them are not identified.',['SA12_Mathew2016','SA12_Stacks0BLZ','SA12_SGA1'],confidence='medium',date=MAT)
add(817,813,[199],'201–205','Alors on peut recoller les (R_i,r_i) …','The draft asserts that local quotient models and coherent overlap equivalences always glue as ramification data with scheme-valued realization. This is false without effective descent of the realized schemes.','false as stated without an effectiveness hypothesis; asserted without proof','C','Stacks03FN provides non-effective étale descent of schemes over a quadratic field extension. Taking trivial local groups gives a direct counterexample at the realization of the terminal object; detailed reduction in proofs.md §4, independently checked by peer31. Corrected scope is CL842.',['SA12_Stacks03FN','SA12_Stacks08KF','SA12_Stacks0BLZ'],comp='No numerical certificate. All local-group, terminal-object and descent hypotheses are checked in proofs.md §4; the non-scheme example and its proof were opened.',present='Unrestricted scheme-valued gluing is refuted. Category/stack descent alone does not ensure that the realization is a scheme.')
add(818,813,[199,200],'207–221','représentation de cette gerbe dans le préchamp Fl_S … r_S est lim→ … tt marche.','A gerbe of local models with a finite étale band and a representation in schemes is used to form a category of equivariant finite covers and a colimit realization. Extending the representation from the model prestack and forming admissible scheme colimits require effectiveness/existence hypotheses.','stated without proof','C','Finite-cover descent is established in Stacks0BLZ. The use of a prestack of schemes cannot be replaced by stackification universality: Stacks03FN obstructs automatic extension of local representations. In the affine Kummer models the relevant quotients exist, but the universal construction is only partially justified as printed.',['SA12_Stacks0BLZ','SA12_Stacks03FN','SA12_Cadman2007'],confidence='high',present='Valid local and affine quotient mechanisms; the arbitrary scheme-valued gerbe-representation construction needs added representability and admissibility hypotheses.')
add(819,814,[200],'222–228','Z_n^a … groupe μ_n … Les axiomes sont vérifiés.','For finitely many nontrivial n_i invertible on S and functions a_i, the affine root chart Spec O_S[t_i]/(t_i^{n_i}−a_i) with its μ_{n_i}-actions gives the asserted Kummer quotient model.','stated without proof','A','Grothendieck–Murre1971 §1 is explicitly Kummer coverings (opened contents and introduction); Borne2009 §2.3.3 writes these same equations, and Cadman Example2.4.1 identifies their quotient stack. No exact equivalence between the draft’s full R1–R4 formalism and all later root-stack formalism is asserted.',['SA12_GrothendieckMurre1971','SA12_Borne2009','SA12_Cadman2007'],comp='Polynomial derivatives n*t^(n−1) and finite-index bookkeeping are redone in outputs/exact_checks.txt; the construction’s descent is conceptual.',date='Grothendieck–Murre publication1971, no earlier circulation evidenced; Cadman arXiv18December2003/publication2007; Borne arXiv10April2007/publication2009.')
add(820,815,[201],'229–239','gerbe abélienne … élément de H² … classes des D_i … comme on devine.','For effective Cartier divisors and globally invertible n_i, the gerbe of local equations with chosen n_i-th-root changes is the product root gerbe of O(D_i). Its neutrality obstruction is (δ[O(D_i)]) in ∏H²_et(S,μ_{n_i}), with the arrow-direction convention recorded.','stated without proof; identification expressly guessed','B','Cadman Example2.4.3 explicitly identifies a line-bundle root gerbe with the H¹→H² Kummer boundary. Stacks03PK gives the exact sequence and topology hypotheses. This directly checks the source’s identification. Grothendieck–Murre §6 is an exact-title historical lead to the related extension/Chern comparison, but only introduction/metadata are used for that book.',['SA12_Cadman2007','SA12_Stacks03PK','SA12_GrothendieckMurre1971'],comp='Čech cocycle and inverse-direction sign identities checked symbolically in outputs/exact_checks.txt; full line-bundle identification in proofs.md §5.',date='Kummer formalism predates these drafts; exact opened root-gerbe account: Cadman first18December2003, publication2007. The draft has no date; this is not a priority claim.')
add(821,814,[200,201],'226–228;240','on passe à la limite sur n variable.','Vary the Kummer exponents through compatible divisibility maps to obtain a limiting ramification theory. A precise modern version is the filtered union of finite-cover categories of root stacks, with inverse limit of their fundamental groups.','stated without proof','B','Borne2009 Proposition6 and §3.2 explicitly prove Rev^D(X)≃colim_r Rev(root_r(D/X)) and the corresponding inverse limit of profinite groups for a connected normal locally noetherian scheme over a field, with simple normal-crossing irreducible divisors and indices prime to the characteristic. These extra hypotheses are retained; the unrestricted source limit is not declared settled by that theorem.',['SA12_Borne2009','SA12_Mathew2016'],confidence='high',date='Borne first arXiv10April2007, revision15February2008; publication Indiana Univ. Math. J.58(1)(2009),137–180. PDF front date27October2021 is a later compilation date.')
add(822,816,[201],'241–244','n_i … premier aux car résiduelles de D_i seulement.','For an effective Cartier divisor D and n invertible along D, the root stack of the pair (O(D),s_D) is Deligne–Mumford even if n is not invertible elsewhere: off D it is the base scheme. A line-bundle root gerbe instead has μ_n inertia everywhere and must use fppf Kummer theory when n is not a global unit.','open on the pages; example2 continuation unfinished','B','Cadman2.3.3 gives the root-stack DM assertion where n is invertible and2.4.2 gives triviality off the divisor; their locality yields the support-only refinement here. Cadman2.4.3 and Stacks03PK distinguish the fppf root gerbe. The refinement is our proof from those exact results, not a verbatim theorem claimed in the paper.',['SA12_Cadman2007','SA12_Stacks03PK'],comp='Example Spec Z,D=(2),n=3 and the root polynomial derivative are recorded in outputs/exact_checks.txt and proofs.md §5.',date='Cadman first2003/publication2007; present support-local inference checked19November2026.')
add(823,[817,818],[202,203,204],'K29-02:245–260;K29-03:2–53','reconstituer … cribles admissibles … R_S=lim→ lim← E.','Given a generating fibred category C, local filteredness and recoverable admissible sieves, the draft proposes reconstructing R as a filtered union of limits of cover categories over s(M); it temporarily requires all φ(M)→1 to be epimorphic.','proof sketched; hypotheses and admissibility self-flagged','C','SGA1 VI gives descent; Mathew5.34–5.36 gives filtered presentations of bounded Galois categories. Neither identifies this exact admissible-sieve relative reconstruction with its arbitrary base and realizations. Source assumptions are not discarded.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Mathew2016','SA12_Stacks0BLZ'],confidence='medium',present='The categorical reconstruction programme is partly developed; the exact theorem requires the missing essential-surjectivity step and precise admissible-sieve data.')
C[-1]['lines']='K29-02_transcription.tex:245–260; K29-03_transcription.tex:2–53'
add(824,819,[204,205,206,207],'45–99','α fidèle; α pleinement fidèle ?','Under the generator-cover assumption and full faithfulness of φ, α detects zero and lifts decompositions by descent. A cancelled independence argument is replaced by local filteredness on207.','proof sketched; revised within the text','C','The graph/subobject route to full faithfulness is standard exact-functor calculus (SGA1 V6), while descent of finite covers is opened in Stacks0BLZ. The argument’s local sections, passage among generators and coherence have been inspected; it is a sketch, not a full published comparison theorem.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BLZ'],confidence='medium',present='Faithfulness is justified by covering generators. Full faithfulness has a viable but compressed descent sketch; the cancelled206 argument is not counted as an additional proof.')
add(825,819,[207],'92–99','Prouvons que α est essentiellement surjectif.','The comparison functor α should be essentially surjective for the specified admissible-sieve reconstruction. The supplied sequence contains no proof after this announcement.','open on the pages; proof only announced','F','No exact theorem with this complete C,s,admissibility setup was identified in searches of SGA, categorical Galois and ramification-data terminology. Modern finite-cover descent and filtered Galois presentations settle related mechanisms, not the missing quantifiers of this α.',['SA12_Stacks0BLZ','SA12_Mathew2016','SA12_SGA1'],confidence='high',present='An open step in the supplied draft. It is not labelled an unsolved modern research problem, since the precise reconstruction hypotheses remain incomplete.')
add(826,821,[209,210,211],'100–181','A) Conditions équivalentes … Isom_ei([1,i],X)=P_i.','Finite locally constant objects of a topos are characterized by a subcanonical finite-cover site and universal effective-descent local finite triviality; on each rank stratum, the symmetric-group frame torsor trivializes an object.','proof sketched','A','Classical finite-cover and descent theory is developed in SGA1 V–VI. Mathew Definition5.15, Proposition5.19 and Theorem5.36 make the globally bounded version precise without a chosen fibre functor. His uniform boundedness is not imposed on all rank-varying objects in the source.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Mathew2016'],confidence='medium',date=SGA)
add(827,822,[209,210,211],'125–181','C équivalente à une catégorie produit ∏_i C_i, les C_i galoisiennes.','With an effective universal decomposition of the terminal object into connected pieces, the finite locally constant category decomposes componentwise into Galois categories; the converse is realized by the sum of the corresponding classifying topoi.','proof sketched; one implication explicitly left to prove','A','SGA1 V9, original printed118, explicitly gives C(S)≃∏C(S_i) for locally noetherian schemes and describes finite continuous functors on the geometric-point groupoid. It leaves general multigaloisian definitions to the reader. This directly matches the product picture, not the disputed G0–G5 implication.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BMQ'],date=SGA)
add(828,822,[210,211,212],'138–193','G0–G5; ramené … X et Y connexes; récurrence sur le majorant le rang.','The source proposes G0–G5 as an axiomatization of the product category. If G5 bounds direct summands over Y′, CountSet satisfies all axioms with connected-rank bound1 but is not Galois; if G5 is interpreted unsliced, CountSet fails it by Hilbert hotel.','proof incomplete; false under the intended slice interpretation; literal unsliced reading unresolved','C','SGA1 V4–5 and Stacks58.3.6 explicitly impose genuine finite-object/fibre conditions. They do not validate this exact connected-only bound. The conditional counterexample and the failure of that example for the unsliced version were independently checked with peer31. No exact later theorem for the ambiguous G5 was found.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BMQ','SA12_Mathew2016'],comp='Full infinitary hypothesis verification in proofs.md §6; no finite computation is advertised as a certificate.',present='The intended rank formulation is insufficient. A reading alarm asks whether the manuscript specifies “over Y′”; no scan decision is made.')
add(829,823,[212],'194–197','il se pourrait que G5 soit surabondant.','The author asks whether G0–G4 already force the needed finiteness/local finite triviality. They do not: the countable-set category satisfies G0–G4 and is not a finite Galois category.','open on the pages; refuted by a present reconstruction','C','The CountSet argument in proofs.md §6 refutes redundancy as a route to the claimed characterization. The standard SGA1/Stacks axioms include finite fibres or equivalent finite decomposition and exactness hypotheses. The author’s reason for dropping the question is unknown.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BMQ'],present='Some substantive finiteness condition is necessary. The exact logical redundancy of every possible unsliced G5 is not replaced by a claim about the intended slice version.')
add(830,824,[213,215],'198–227;312–326','X′≃Y′⊔X′_1; degré n−1; Y″→Y … composé … OK.','Finite limits, universal complements of monomorphisms, and the displayed rank axioms imply local finite triviality by induction on degree. Pulling back X→Y along itself splits off its diagonal, leaving degree n−1, which is trivialized inductively.','proved there modulo the stated degree/descent axioms','A','The source’s induction has been checked. It is the finite-cover splitting argument underlying SGA1 V and is reflected in Mathew5.28’s rank induction. The total pullback degree must read n; only its complement has degree n−1.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Mathew2016'],comp='Degree arithmetic checked for n1–7 in outputs/exact_checks.txt; the proof is categorical and retained in proofs.md.',date=SGA)
add(831,824,[215],'327–336','il suffit … épimorphisme effectif universel … relations d’équivalence effectives universelles.','In a finitely complete exact setting, a universally effective epimorphism has effective descent: a descent datum supplies an equivalence relation whose universal effective quotient is the descended object.','proof sketched by a diagram','B','The quotient construction is the standard effective-descent argument. Mathew5.19–5.22 matches it in Galois categories; Reiterman–Sobral–Tholen1993 studies composition under explicit category hypotheses. No claim that every effective epimorphism in an arbitrary category has effective descent is made.',['SA12_Mathew2016','SA12_ReitermanSobralTholen1993'],confidence='high',date='General categorical descent reference publication1993; its introduction reports studies begunNovember1991, not a public preprint date. Mathew first2014/publication2016.')
add(832,824,[215],'338–339','degré n … uniquement déterminée … X×_Y Y′≃[1,n]_{Y′}.','Under the full degree axioms, degree n is characterized by becoming the n-fold constant cover after a universal effective-descent cover; hence the degree assignment is unique.','stated as corollary of the preceding proof','A','This is the usual local rank of finite locally constant objects (SGA1 V; Stacks0BL6). The trivializing cover is essential, and the preceding induction supplies it.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BL6'],date=SGA)
add(833,825,[214],'228–263;304–311','dim n−(cardP−1)d ≥ n−card(E)d; égalité … P_0; dim F_3=1.','Equality partitions of a three-element set have block counts3,2,1. Under transverse multiple-point hypotheses the expected dimension for m distinct points is n−(m−1)d. Thus the claimed sharp bound must be n−(|E|−1)d; for n3,d1 the triple and double strata have dimensions1 and2.','cancelled computation; sharp-bound and partition-label errors as transcribed','F','Modern multiple-point literature uses the same expected-dimension count, but the source’s “net” condition and F-spaces are not defined here. The arithmetic and partition decomposition were independently checked; no theorem about its unspecified closure spaces is identified.',['SA12_MultiplePointsSearch'],confidence='high',comp='All five partitions and dimensions, including the off-by-d difference, enumerated in outputs/exact_checks.txt.',present='The dimension arithmetic raises concrete scan alarms; the transverse interpretation is a stated reconstruction, not an unconditional assertion about f.')
add(834,825,[214],'265–294','Question … f net … Mais que peut-on dire de …','The cancelled leaf asks whether closures of strict triple-point strata meet the full diagonal or pair-collision strata, under an undefined “net” hypothesis.','open on the pages; questions in cancelled working block','F','Searches for multiple-point spaces, expected dimensions and the source terminology did not identify the precise quantifiers. The other supplied occurrence “f est net en x” is a hypothesis at C29-05 scan114:83 and supplies no definition. Modern multiple-point results cannot be substituted without it.',['SA12_MultiplePointsSearch'],confidence='high',present='Not a well-identified modern open problem. Recover the meaning of net and the ambient F_2/F_3 spaces before assigning a theorem or counterexample.')
add(835,826,[216],'340–359','F … conservatif, exact à gauche et commute aux sommes finies … C≃C(π).','G0–G3 together with a conservative finite-limit- and finite-coproduct-preserving functor F:C→FinSet imply finite frame torsor trivializations, universal effective descent, finite-colimit exactness and the usual connected Galois-category classification.','stated without proof','A','SGA1 V4.1 and Stacks58.3.6–10 give the standard exact-functor classification. The weaker printed F condition is sufficient under this stronger G3: the independent frame proof in proofs.md §7 supplies the omitted step. Mathew5.15,5.19 supports the local-triviality-to-finite-colimits argument.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BMQ','SA12_Mathew2016'],comp='Frame cardinalities and small symmetric-group action bijections checked in outputs/exact_checks.txt; conservativity and universal-descent proof in proofs.md §7.',date=SGA,present='The statement is supported by a complete reconstruction; it should not be marked false merely because quotient preservation of F is not separately listed.')
add(836,827,[217],'360–366','u fidèle ⇔ u conservatif ⇔ u(X)=∅⇒X=∅.','For an exact functor between the stated finite-cover categories, detecting the initial object, faithfulness and conservativity are equivalent, via equalizers, image decompositions and complements.','stated without proof','A','SGA1 V6 gives the exact-functor formalism. We re-prove the equivalences directly in proofs.md §8; in the connected case they also follow from the group-homomorphism description in Stacks58.3.11.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BMQ'],date=SGA)
add(837,827,[217],'367–372','pleinement fidèle ⇔ bijection … sous-objets de X … ss-objets de Y.','After correcting the target mathematically to u(X), an exact functor is fully faithful iff it induces bijections Sub(X)→Sub(uX). Full faithfulness lifts characteristic maps to1⊔1; the converse lifts graphs and uses conservativity.','stated without proof; target reading doubtful','A','This is the finite-cover exact-functor criterion of SGA1 V6 and the graph proof familiar from Stacks58.3.10. Our complete Boolean-category argument is in proofs.md §8. The ink is not changed.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BMQ'],date=SGA)
add(838,[807,817,828],[193,202,217],'K29-02:109–125;245–260;K29-03:370–374','u équivalence ⇔ … tout objet … subordonné à u(X).','With the literal193 definition and no covering requirement on u(X), the essential-surjectivity criterion and subordinate-closure margin are false. Every object is subordinate to0, but the trivial-action inclusion FinSet→Fin(C2)-Set is exact fully faithful and not essentially surjective.','false as stated under the literal definition','C','The counterexample is independently verified in proofs.md §8; standard Galois descent requires an effective cover. A covering condition is explicitly inserted in the manuscript’s definition of generators on202. The safe refinement is CL840.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BMQ','SA12_Mathew2016'],comp='Finite trivial-action Hom counts and the regular C2-set obstruction checked in outputs/exact_checks.txt; zero-pullback argument in proofs.md §8.',present='Literal assertion refuted. Whether a cover was an intended standing convention is a scan/context question, not assumed.')
C[-1]['lines']='K29-02_transcription.tex:109–125;245–260; K29-03_transcription.tex:370–374'
add(839,808,[194],'126–131','Supposons R1 R3 R4. Alors R2 équivaut … r fidèle.','The cancelled implication R2⇒faithful realization is false even when R1,R3,R4 hold: finite C2-sets realized by their orbit schemes satisfy all four conditions and identify two distinct automorphisms of the regular C2-set.','false as stated; cancelled in the same text','F','No exact later publication of this cancelled equivalence was identified. The counterexample is this run’s reconstruction, independently checked by peer31. It illustrates why the full-faithfulness criterion for exact functors cannot be applied to a realization that is only right exact. Refined statement CL837 applies to exact functors, not r.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BMQ'],comp='Regular C2-set equivariant automorphisms and invariant subsets checked exactly in outputs/exact_checks.txt; whole étale-site and R1–R4 proof in proofs.md §3.',present='Refuted as a mathematical assertion; cancellation status is explicit, but the author’s reason for cancelling is unknown.')
add(840,828,[202,217],'K29-02:245–260;K29-03:370–374','Covering condition appears at202, but not in the217 equivalence criterion.','Reconstruction: for connected Galois categories, or componentwise products with effective gluing, an exact fully faithful functor is an equivalence if every target object is subordinate to a covering image object. More generally, uniformly bounded piecewise-constant descent data suffice; arbitrary unbounded gluing must be an explicit closure hypothesis.','refinement reconstructed in this run; not a new manuscript assertion','A','SGA1 V–VI Galois descent and Mathew5.32,5.40 provide the covering/torsor mechanisms. The scope qualification avoids treating arbitrary finite-locally-constant categories with different unbounded-gluing conventions as identical.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Mathew2016'],confidence='medium',date=SGA,present='A precise repaired criterion is available in the standard Galois/product setting. The literal source is recorded separately in CL838.')
C[-1]['lines']='K29-02_transcription.tex:245–260; K29-03_transcription.tex:370–374'
add(841,823,[212],'195–197','G5′ … F exact … F(e_i)≠∅.','The proposed replacement uses an exact finite-set functor nonzero on each connected terminal component. With the stipulated effective component decomposition, its restriction to that component detects nonempty objects by G3, hence is conservative, and the finite-functor theorem applies componentwise.','stated without proof','C','The connected exact-functor classification is SGA1 V4.1/Stacks58.3.6–10. The componentwise argument supplies the proposed repair when G4 means effective descent along the components; the exact G0–G5′ package was not located as a published axiom list. The equality-to-empty branch would not express this condition.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Stacks0BMQ'],confidence='medium',date=SGA)
add(842,813,[199,200],'201–221','Scheme-valued gluing and gerbe colimits are asserted.','Refinement: descend the categories and finite covers, and require effective descent of their scheme realizations and existence of admissible quotient colimits; alternatively work with algebraic-space-valued realizations. The finite affine Kummer models meet the relevant effective representability requirements.','refinement reconstructed in this run; not a manuscript theorem','B','Stacks03FN proves why scheme representability cannot be omitted; Stacks0BLZ supplies effective finite étale descent. Cadman’s affine quotient charts verify the stated Kummer local model. This is the precise restriction attached to CL817–818.',['SA12_Stacks03FN','SA12_Stacks0BLZ','SA12_Cadman2007'],confidence='high',present='The geometric effectiveness hypothesis repairs the identified obstruction; it does not by itself prove every unprinted coherence assertion of the general draft.')
add(843,803,[191],'73–74','Dans R4, il suffirait de supposer Y=e.','The rank-cover condition for general maps reduces to the terminal-object rank covers by jointly decomposing the base into global rank strata for X and Y; on each such piece there are only finitely many possible relative ranks, and finite coproducts are preserved.','stated without proof in a margin','A','This is a formal finite-rank reduction in the finite-cover calculus, consistent with SGA1 V and the rank arguments of Mathew5.28. The required pullback compatibility is only along the complementary base pieces (CL804), not arbitrary products.',['SA12_SGA1','SA12_Mathew2016'],confidence='medium',date=SGA)
